---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-hearing-loss`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Hearing Loss',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Hearing Loss', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Hearing Loss: Cochlear Otoconia, BPPV, and Presbycusis"
  description="What the evidence shows for vitamin D and hearing — BPPV recurrence, sudden sensorineural hearing loss, age-related presbycusis, and otoconia calcium biology."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-hearing-loss", label: "Hearing Loss" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Hearing Loss</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin D and calcium metabolism are relevant to inner-ear function in
      three specific ways: benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV) recurrence,
      sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSNHL), and age-related presbycusis.
      Evidence is strongest for BPPV.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">BPPV — the calcium otoconia link</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      BPPV is caused by dislodged otoconia — calcium carbonate crystals — floating
      into the semicircular canals. Vitamin D and calcium metabolism affect otoconia
      formation and stability. A 2020 South Korean randomised trial (Jeong) of
      957 BPPV patients showed vitamin D 400 IU + calcium 500 mg reduced BPPV
      recurrence rate by ~24% over 12 months (annualised recurrence 0.83 vs 1.10
      episodes). Reasonable adjunct in recurrent BPPV.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSNHL)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Small observational studies find vitamin D-deficient SSNHL patients have
      poorer recovery than replete patients. Corticosteroids remain the standard
      early treatment. Vitamin D correction is not established as changing
      outcomes but is reasonable general care.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Presbycusis and age-related hearing loss</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cross-sectional studies find modest correlation between low 25(OH)D and
      hearing threshold in older adults, but Mendelian randomisation and
      supplementation trials do not consistently support causation. Age-related
      hearing loss is multifactorial (noise exposure, ototoxic medications,
      vascular changes, genetics).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guide</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Recurrent BPPV: check 25(OH)D and calcium; consider 800 IU vitamin D + 500 mg calcium daily as adjunct to Epley/Semont maneuvers.</li>
      <li>SSNHL: prompt otolaryngology evaluation (steroids are time-sensitive within days); vitamin D adjunct is reasonable.</li>
      <li>General age-related hearing changes: get audiology assessment; vitamin D unlikely to reverse but useful for general health.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Sudden hearing loss is a medical
      emergency — steroid treatment window is only days for best recovery.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-hearing-loss" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
